## Sudo Privileges

**Why sudo access is important:**
- Enhances security by limiting direct root usage
- Provides accountability through command logging
- Allows controlled and temporary elevated access
- Prevents accidental system damage from constant root login

---

## File Permissions

### Common Permission Examples
- **777** — Everyone has full read, write, and execute access (insecure)
- **755** — Owner has full access, group and others can read + execute
- **644** — Owner can read and write, group and others can only read
- **600** — Owner has full read/write access, completely private to owner

---

## File Ownership
- `chown` — Changes ownership of files and directories (user and group)

## Permission Modification
- `chmod` — Changes file and directory permissions (symbolic or numeric mode)

